  </div>

  <h2>Using both on the same folder</h2>
  <p>This works today, and it's an easy way to try Noughtpad without moving anything.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>In Noughtpad, open <strong>Settings → Notes folder → Switch…</strong> and choose your Obsidian vault. Or open your Noughtpad folder as a vault in Obsidian.</li>
    <li>Write in either app. Changes made in one show up in the other straight away.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>A few things to know:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Noughtpad reads every Markdown file in the folder, including ones with no header.</li>
    <li>New notes from Noughtpad go into year and month folders, like <code>2026/11/</code>, with a short header holding the date and labels. Obsidian shows Noughtpad's labels as a property called <code>labels</code>, not as Obsidian tags.</li>
    <li>Noughtpad doesn't turn <code>[[links]]</code> into clickable links. They stay as text.</li>
    <li>Noughtpad keeps its index in a hidden <code>.noughtpad</code> folder, which Obsidian ignores.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Questions people ask</h2>
  <h3>Is Noughtpad a replacement for Obsidian?</h3>
  <p>
    For some people. If you mostly write and want the app to find connections for you, Noughtpad may be all you need. If you rely on plugins, hand-made links or a phone app, keep Obsidian, and try Noughtpad alongside it on the same folder.
  </p>
  <h3>Can I move from Obsidian to Noughtpad?</h3>
  <p>There's nothing to move. Point Noughtpad at your vault and it reads your notes where they are.</p>
  <h3>Does Noughtpad send my notes to an AI service?</h3>
  <p>
    No, not unless you add an online provider for chat yourself, and even then it shows you which notes would be sent and asks first. Search by meaning, related notes and ideas over time all run on your computer.
  </p>

</CompareLayout>
